A facade can re-export declarations from another source file or package without defining a wrapper. Place `$export` immediately before a direct-selection dependency clause in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth`.

### Direct-selection re-exports

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    @components/card CardData as Card, render_card

    $export
    @core/math PI, sin
]

This publishes `Card`, `render_card`, `PI` and `sin` through the facade. `CardData` remains available under its original name only if the facade also selects it without an alias. A leaf `as` alias is both the local selected name and the public name.

One directive applies to all names on the following clause. An ordinary unmarked dependency clause stays private:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components/card CardData as LocalCard
]

`LocalCard` is now bound in this file, but is not public.

### Selection syntax

The provider path and first selected name must begin on the same physical line. A comma continues the list across a newline:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    @components/card CardData,
        render_card
]

Without a comma, a newline ends the clause. A trailing comma is invalid because another selected name is required.

Re-exports require a nonempty list of flat, explicit selections. Whole namespaces, namespace aliases, nested selections, wildcard exports and export lists are unsupported. A function or receiver method cannot be re-exported by inventing a name independent of an existing selected binding. Receiver methods remain attached to their receiver type.

### Public identity and validation

Re-exports preserve the selected declaration's origin, type and callable error contract. They cannot expose an unknown or inaccessible private declaration. Exported names must be unique across direct declarations and re-exported selections in one facade.

A private callable's inferred implicit failure cannot escape through a public callable without an appropriate declared error slot or local handling. See @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API) for the public contract.

